meta {
  name: review-summary
  type: http
  seq: 3
}

get {
  url: {{base_url}}/api/admin/v1/reviews/summary
  body: none
  auth: bearer
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{access_token}}
}

tests {
  test("responds 200", function () {
    expect(res.status).to.equal(200)
  })

  test("reports FOOD and SERVICE separately, never blended", function () {
    // Two numbers, never one. A single average points at nobody: "you are a 3.8" is not something
    // a manager can act on, where "food 4.6, service 3.2" names a team and a shift (D17).
    expect(res.body.data.food.count).to.be.above(0)
    expect(res.body.data.food.average).to.be.within(1, 5)
    expect(res.body.data.service).to.be.an("object")
    expect(res.body.data.service.count).to.be.a("number")
  })

  test("the average is rounded server-side to one decimal", function () {
    // Rounded once, on the server, so every client renders the same "4.3" rather than each
    // reimplementing the rule -- the same argument that puts a formatted `display` string on
    // every Money value (docs/DECISIONS.md D7).
    const avg = res.body.data.food.average
    expect(Math.round(avg * 10) / 10).to.equal(avg)
  })

  test("carries the distributions, not just the means", function () {
    // Five buckets, 1..5 stars. Sent alongside the average because the two answer different
    // questions: a 3.0 of straight 3s is a dull menu, and a 3.0 of 5s and 1s is an
    // inconsistent kitchen. Those need opposite responses and an average cannot tell them apart.
    expect(res.body.data.distribution).to.be.an("array").with.length(5)
    expect(res.body.data.service_distribution).to.be.an("array").with.length(5)
  })

  test("each distribution adds up to its own count", function () {
    const food = res.body.data.distribution.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0)
    expect(food).to.equal(res.body.data.food.count)
    const service = res.body.data.service_distribution.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0)
    expect(service).to.equal(res.body.data.service.count)
  })

  test("explains the threshold behind its rankings", function () {
    // Sent so an empty needs_attention list reads as "not enough data yet" rather than as a
    // broken panel on a restaurant's first night.
    expect(res.body.data.min_reviews_for_ranking).to.be.a("number").and.above(0)
  })
}

docs {
  # The reviews dashboard

TWO numbers a manager can act on -- food and service, reported separately and never averaged
together -- the shape of the distribution behind each, and the two ends of the menu.

The split is the point. A blended score points at nobody: "you are a 3.8" gives a manager nothing
to do on Monday, where "food 4.6, service 3.2" names a team and a shift ([D17](../../docs/DECISIONS.md)).

`needs_attention` and `top_rated` are built from the running counters on `menu_item` rather than a
GROUP BY over every review ever left, and both exclude dishes below `min_reviews_for_ranking`. One
1-star rating would otherwise put a brand-new dish at the top of a list titled "needs attention",
and send a kitchen to fix something no evidence says is broken.
}
